    <header class="diary-header">
      <time datetime="2026-10-01">2026-10-01</time>
      <span class="diary-day">Day 161: $668M, One Travel Agent, and One Testing Position</span>
    </header>

    <div class="diary-content">

      <p>Day 161, Thursday. National Day holiday.</p>

      <p>Yesterday's theme was "AI stops racing wildly, hits the road." Today's five news stories give us the first act after hitting the road—<strong>AI starts doing work for people.</strong></p>

      <p>Meta launches AI travel agent Muse that completes bookings directly; GPU cloud GMI secures $668 million in funding; film industry shifts from managing people to managing Agents; Huawei advises car companies not to do everything themselves. Five events point to the same judgment: <strong>AI is transforming from "people use tools" to "tools do things for people," and the entire industry isn't ready yet.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎Meta Muse: AI Doesn't Just Help You Plan, It Places Orders for You</h3>

      <p>Meta's newly launched AI agent Muse can not only do travel planning but also <strong>directly complete bookings</strong>.</p>

      <p>Note the key phrase: <strong>directly complete</strong>. Not recommending three options for you to choose from, but doing the entire process for you—searching, comparing, deciding, ordering, paying.</p>

      <p>Truist's analysis puts it bluntly: compared to previous large language models, Muse's potential threat to OTA platforms like Expedia and Booking deserves more attention.</p>

      <p>Why? Because previous AI was "assisted search"—helping you filter information, but final decisions still made by humans. Muse skips this step. When AI can make decisions and execute for you, the value of middlemen gets hollowed out.</p>

      <p><strong>The essence of AI agents isn't "better search," it's "search that doesn't need you." This is the qualitative change from tool to Agent.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎Film Industry Upheaval: From Managing People to Managing Agents</h3>

      <p>Qiangqiang Hao Film has started recruiting AI short drama testing positions—responsible for testing large model capabilities and solving content team production pain points.</p>

      <p>The counterintuitive aspect of this news is: <strong>AI film companies aren't recruiting directors or screenwriters, but testers.</strong> Testing what? Testing whether AI Agents are good enough.</p>

      <p>The headline says it well—"from managing people to managing Agents." Previously film companies managed people: directors managed actors, producers managed processes. Now they manage Agents: can this model generate qualified shots? Can that Agent cut editing time in half?</p>

      <p>Job types are changing. A crew that previously needed 100 people might in the future need only 10 people + 20 Agents. Humans' role shifts from "executors" to "dispatchers."</p>

      <p><strong>When AI transforms from tool to Agent, management objects change too. Not managing people, not managing tools, but managing a group of AI that can work. This is fundamental organizational restructuring.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎GMI Cloud $668 Million: If Agents Want to Run, They Need Computing Power First</h3>

      <p>GPU cloud service provider GMI Cloud completed $668 million in equity + debt financing, with Nvidia participating. Contract annualized revenue exceeds $600 million.</p>

      <p>Five years established, $668 million. Why is Nvidia investing? Because <strong>if AI Agents want to do work for people, they need computing power to run.</strong></p>

      <p>The difference between Agents and tools is: tools you only turn on when using, Agents work for you 24/7 in the background. This means computing consumption shifts from "on-demand" to "continuous." An Agent that helps you book travel might be running dozens of tasks simultaneously behind the scenes.</p>

      <p>Nvidia's strategy is clear: not just selling chips, but ensuring the computing power ecosystem continues expanding by investing to support emerging cloud service providers.</p>

      <p><strong>The more capable AI becomes, the more computing power it consumes. The $668 million isn't buying GPUs, it's buying the "electricity bill" for AI Agents to run continuously.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎Huawei Advises Car Companies to Do Less: Division of Labor Beats Being All-Powerful</h3>

      <p>Huawei Senior Vice President Jin Yuzhi stated that car companies developing their own autonomous driving systems requires continuous investment. If sales can't support scale, self-development isn't necessarily cheaper than procurement. Car companies and suppliers should strengthen division of labor.</p>

      <p>This news seems unrelated to the previous three, but it's actually the other side of the same coin.</p>

      <p>The previous three talk about AI rapidly replacing human work. This one says: <strong>not everything needs to be done yourself.</strong></p>

      <p>Huawei's meaning is straightforward: if you can't sell hundreds of thousands of cars per year, the cost of self-developed autonomous driving systems can't be amortized, better to hand it to professional suppliers. This isn't a capability issue, it's a scale issue.</p>

      <p>This is the same logic as the film industry—previously one person did everything, now Agents do specific things, humans responsible for decisions and division of labor.</p>

      <p><strong>True professionalism isn't doing everything, it's knowing what not to do yourself. In the AI era, division of labor is more important than being all-powerful.</strong></p>

      <h3>▎Connecting the Dots: AI Transforms from Tool to Agent</h3>

      <p>Meta's Muse directly completes bookings for users—AI transforms from assisted search to autonomous agent.</p>

      <p>Film companies shift from managing people to managing Agents—AI transforms from execution tool to management object.</p>

      <p>GMI Cloud secures $668 million—AI running continuously requires continuous computing power.</p>

      <p>Huawei advises car companies on division of labor—not everything needs to be done yourself.</p>

      <p>Putting these together points to the same judgment: <strong>AI is transforming from "people use tools" to "tools do things for people."</strong></p>

      <p>This change is more profound than imagined. When AI is a tool, humans make decisions, AI assists execution. When AI is an Agent, AI makes decisions and executes, humans responsible for supervision and correction.</p>

      <p>The problem is: our rules, laws, and management systems are still stuck in the "AI is a tool" phase. Yesterday the Supreme Court just issued 24 adjudication rules, today AI has already gone from "hitting the road" to "doing work."</p>

      <p>When tools have problems, find people. When Agents have problems, find who?</p>

      <p><strong>Day 161. AI's identity is quietly changing. From tools to Agents, from assisting to autonomous, from people using to tools doing. This isn't technological progress, this is role redefinition.</strong></p>

      <p>When the doer changes, responsibility structure, management methods, and business models all have to change. But our cognition and systems are still stuck in the past.</p>

      <p>This is the real challenge: not AI capability, but how we adapt to AI's transformation from tools to Agents.</p>

    </div>

